# DESCRIPTION

VectraOS is not Unix, but it runs Unix programs, as Plan 9's APE does: in an environment of their own, made of a C library that speaks POSIX above and VectraOS below, and a namespace arranged as such programs expect.
It is the one compatibility layer the system has (rule 13 of `docs/00-overview.md`), and it lives entirely inside the C library and the namespace: the kernel and the servers know nothing of POSIX beyond what any client may use.

A POSIX program is a C program built against musl 1.2.6, vendored unchanged (ADR-0007), and VectraOS's back end, `ports/musl/vx`, which answers musl's system calls.
musl asks for the kernel by Linux's system-call numbers, with Linux's structures and errors; the back end answers each with VectraOS's own calls and servers: files through the process's namespace, over 9Px; processes and signals through procfs(4) and notes; terminals through ptyd(4); sockets through netd(4); memory through VMOs.
What it does not do yet answers `ENOSYS`, and is reported once, by number, to the kernel log.
Programs are linked statically, for the target <arch>`-vectra-unknown-musl`, which defines neither `__linux__` nor `__unix__`.

A POSIX program and a native one are both ordinary processes: each is spawned the same way, sees a namespace, has a pid in the one process table, and can be piped to the other.

@node=namespace title="The POSIX namespace"

A POSIX program expects names a native program does without: `/dev/null`, `/tmp`, `/bin` with its commands.
The namespace template `/lib/ns/posix` (see namespace(6) and namespace(7)) gives them, and a service manifest's `ns=posix` gives a service that namespace (see svc(6)):

```text
mount /srv/bootfs /
bind -a /boot/bin /bin
bind -b /boot/bin/posix /bin
mount -c /srv/tmpfs /tmp
mount /srv/null /dev
mount -a /srv/ptyd /dev
mount /srv/proc /proc
mount /srv/net /net
mount /srv/sys /sys
```

`/bin` is a union with `/boot/bin/posix` first, then the boot image's own `/bin` and `/boot/bin`, as Plan 9 binds `/bin/ape` over `/bin`: a POSIX program looking up `ls` finds sbase's, and a native program, whose `/bin` has only `/boot/bin`, finds the native one.
Nothing is renamed, and one page in section 1 describes both (see intro(1)).
`/tmp` is tmpfs(4), `/dev` nullfs(4)'s `null`, `zero`, `random` and `urandom` with ptyd's `ptmx` and `pts/` after them, `/proc` procfs, `/net` netd and `/sys` sysfs(4), for the clock.

The console shell's namespace is not this one: it has no `/dev` and no `/boot/bin/posix` on `/bin`, so a POSIX program run from it finds less than it expects.

@node=library title="musl and the back end"

The kernel starts a program with one channel holding its spawn message (svc(6)); the back end's `crt1.o` reads it and builds what musl's start expects of a Linux stack: the arguments, the environment, and the auxiliary vector, whose `AT_RANDOM` comes from the seed the parent gave (`entropy`).
A program given no seed has an unseeded generator, and `getrandom` fails rather than pretend.

The descriptor table lives in the process, as Fuchsia's fdio keeps it: a descriptor names an open file description, the console, an end of a pipe, a file or directory, a terminal or a socket.
The namespace is built from the spawn message the first time a name is used.
The current directory is the back end's too, a path it puts in front of each relative name, since the namespace itself takes only absolute ones.

The process's identity is its task: `getpid` is the task's id, which `exec` keeps (ADR-0011, ADR-0012); `uname` says `VectraOS`.
Time is the kernel's monotonic clock, and the realtime clocks add the kernel's offset from UTC, which devmgr sets from the hardware clock at boot, on x86_64 (see time(7)).

@node=files title="Files and descriptors"

`open`, `read`, `write`, `stat` and the rest resolve a name through the namespace to a server, then speak 9Px to it over that mount's ring (see rings(7)): walk(5), open(5), read(5), getattr(5) and the rest.
`rename`, `symlink`, locks and `fsync` use the `posix` extension's messages, 9P2000.L's, on a server that has it: tmpfs, fsd(4) and dosfs(4).

An open file's offset and `O_APPEND` belong to the open file description, which POSIX shares between a process and its children, and between descriptors made by `dup`.
On a server with `posix`, the server keeps them: reads and writes go at the server's offset, `lseek` is `Tseek`, and appends from two processes are atomic.
A child, by `fork`, `posix_spawn` or `exec`, joins the same open file with a token from share(5), on a connection of its own; so `(a; b) > f` writes `f` once through, as a Unix shell's would.
On a server without `posix`, bootfs or u9fs, a child opens the file again, with an offset of its own.

A pipe is a channel between the processes, each write one message of up to 4 KiB, the same pipes native programs' standard input and output use, so a POSIX program and a native one can be piped together.
The reader sees the end of the file when the last writer anywhere has closed.

`mmap` of a file on a server with the `map` extension, fsd, maps the VMO map(5) gives: the file's page cache, which every process mapping it shares, so `MAP_SHARED` writes reach the file and the others.
A private mapping that may be written, or one of another server's file, is a copy of the file's bytes, and a shared mapping of another server's file is refused.
Anonymous memory is a VMO of its own.

@node=processes title="fork, exec and the process calls"

`fork` makes a task with a copy of the caller's memory and handles, made at once (see task(2)).
The rings of the parent's connections are not shared, since a ring has one producer: the child lets them go, opens connections of its own, builds its namespace again (in its namespace group, through a channel of its own to nsd(4)), and joins its open files by token.
`exec` loads the new program into a scratch task and moves it into the caller with `task_exec`, so the process keeps its task, and with it its pid, its parent and its children; descriptors cross it as they cross a spawn, all but those marked close-on-exec.
`posix_spawn` builds the child directly, never cloning the parent, and registers it with procfs before it runs.

Every other process call is a file in `/proc`, as APE's are (ADR-0011): `getppid` reads `/proc/`<pid>`/ppid`, `getpgrp` and `setpgid` read and write `noteid`, since a process group is a note group, `setsid` writes `ctl`, and `waitpid` reads `/proc/`<pid>`/wait`, keeping a record for a child it was not asked about.
Without `/proc` in its namespace a process is alone: no parent, group or children.

`exit(0)` ends the process with an empty exit string, and `exit(`<n>`)` with <n> in decimal; `wait` turns them back, and turns a note that ended the process into the signal that killed it (see notes(7)).

@node=signals title="Signals"

Signals are built on notes, as 9front's APE builds them (ADR-0010).
`kill` writes a note to `/proc/`<pid>`/note`, or to `notepg` for a group, which procfs posts: `posix: SIGTERM pid=12`, or Plan 9's own words where Plan 9 has a note for the signal, `interrupt` for `SIGINT`, `hangup`, `alarm`, `sys: write on closed pipe`.
The back end's note handler maps the note back to its signal, and a fault, which arrives as a note in Plan 9's words such as `sys: trap: fault read addr=… pc=…`, to `SIGSEGV`, `SIGBUS`, `SIGILL`, `SIGFPE` or `SIGTRAP`.
A note that is no signal ends the process with it, as in Plan 9.

The dispositions, the mask, the pending set and `SA_RESTART` live in the back end.
A handler never runs inside the back end, which may be in the middle of a ring submission: a signal that arrives there is made pending, the call returns, and the signal is delivered on the way out, with `EINTR` for a handler without `SA_RESTART`.
A stopping signal stops the process through its `ctl`, and `SIGKILL`, `SIGSTOP` and `SIGCONT` from another process procfs carries out itself.
`SIGCHLD` is a note procfs posts to a parent when a child ends, stops or goes on.

@node=terminals title="Terminals"

ptyd(4) serves `/dev/ptmx` and `/dev/pts/`<n>: opening `ptmx` makes a terminal, and the program on it reads and writes its slave.
The line discipline is in ptyd, with termios's settings in Linux's numbers; `tcgetattr`, `tcsetattr`, the window size and `tcsetpgrp` read and write the terminal's `ctl` file.
`^C`, `^\` and `^Z` are notes written to the foreground group's `notepg`, so they reach every process in it.

A program whose standard input and output are the console, a handle its spawn message gave it, sees a terminal with no termios: the UART driver's cooked console (see services(7)).

@node=sockets title="Sockets"

The BSD socket calls are files in `/net`, as APE has them.
`socket` opens `/net/tcp/clone` or `/net/udp/clone` and then the conversation's `data` file, which the descriptor holds; `bind` and `listen` write `announce` to its `ctl`, `connect` writes `connect`, `accept` opens `listen`, and `getsockname` and `getpeername` read `local` and `remote`.
A UDP socket reads and writes after netd's header, so `sendto` and `recvfrom` carry addresses.
A socket waits as a terminal does, with a read kept outstanding on a connection of its own, so `poll`, `select`, `O_NONBLOCK` and `EINTR` work on it.
A `fork` or `exec` opens the conversation's `data` again by its path.

@node=commands title="The commands"

The POSIX commands are sbase's, at a pinned commit, vendored unchanged (ADR-0016): one binary, `sbase-box`, in `/boot/bin/posix` with each command's name as a link to it, and `make` beside it.
lua(1) is a POSIX program too (ADR-0015).
Each command has its page in section 1, which says where it falls short; much of that is this layer's, below.

# EXAMPLES

A test's manifest, `tests/user/ctest.ndb`, its comment left out: a POSIX program, with arguments, an environment, the POSIX namespace and nsd's post:

```ndb
service=ctest program=/boot/bin/ctest console entropy
arg=one
arg="two words"
env=GREETING=hello
ns=posix
connect=nsd
```

and its lines from the POSIX test's log:

```text
ctest: hello from musl
ctest: argv one|two words
ctest: to stderr
ctest: 340 checks, 0 failed
ctest: atexit ran
```

