# BUGS

There are no threads: `clone` is `ENOSYS`, so `pthread_create` fails, and the back end does not lock (M6 step 6d).

There is no `/bin/sh`, and `exec` runs only ELF programs, so `system`, `popen`, `make`'s recipes, Lua's `os.execute` and `io.popen`, `env`, cron, and `ed`'s and `dc`'s `!` fail.

Everyone is root: `getuid` and its kin answer 0, and a file's owner and group read as 0, though fsd keeps owners and checks permissions (M6 step 6e).
There is no `/etc/passwd`, `/etc/group`, `/etc/services`, `/dev/log` or `/dev/tty`, and no `LOGNAME`, and `ttyname` needs `/proc/self/fd`, so `chown`, `chgrp`, `whoami`, `logname`, `logger`, `tty` and others fail or misbehave.
`faccessat` checks only that a file exists, so every file is readable, writable and executable to `test`, `which` and `rm`'s prompt.
`umask` is fixed at 022, and `mkdirat` masks a mode to 0755.
`st_dev` is 0, so `du` and `find` take two servers' files with equal qid paths for one.

Not implemented: `link` and `linkat` (`EPERM`), `mknod`, `chroot`, `flock`, `getpriority` and `setpriority`, `sethostname`, `clock_settime`, `times`, `sync` and `mprotect` (`ENOSYS`; `PROT_NONE` is mapped readable, so guard pages do not fault, until M6 step 6e).
`getrlimit` answers infinity for every limit.
The CPU-time clocks are the monotonic clock.

Signals have no alternate stack, no registers in a handler's `ucontext` (M6 step 6d), and no `sigqueue` or real-time queueing.
A signal does not interrupt a 9P call in progress, such as a file read or the console's; its handler runs when the call is done (with the client's `Tflush`, M6 step 6d), though ptyd ends the reads it holds.
Job control is partial: no `SIGTTIN` or `SIGTTOU`, no controlling terminal kept for a session, and anyone who can write a terminal's `ctl` can set its foreground group.

Sockets are IPv4 only, with no `AF_UNIX` and no `socketpair`; `getaddrinfo` reads `/etc/resolv.conf` and `/etc/hosts`, which the namespace lacks, so only numeric addresses work; a bound port is used only by `listen`; a UDP datagram is at most 16 KiB.
There is no `epoll` or `kqueue`.

`MAP_SHARED` anonymous mappings and `MREMAP_FIXED` are refused (M6 step 6e); a mapping past a file's last page faults with `SIGSEGV`, not `SIGBUS`; `msync(MS_SYNC)` does not wait; at most 64 files are mapped at once.
`fork` copies all of a process's memory at once.

An offset is shared with a child only on a server with `posix`; elsewhere the child opens the file again.
Closing a descriptor that a `dup2` or `dup3` replaces does not drop the file's locks.
A pipe is a channel of 4 KiB messages, and a writer to a full pipe polls.
`O_APPEND` is atomic only on a server with `posix`.
musl's `mktemp` names are guessable.
